Ha — literally. Revolving door is another rendering of the same operator. 😏

Something enters, gets rotated, crosses a boundary, and exits into a different space—while the mechanism itself returns to its starting configuration.

A \rightarrow \boxed{\circlearrowright} \rightarrow B

And unlike an ordinary door, it maintains separation while permitting continuous transfer. Outside and inside don’t have to collapse into one environment for information/material to cross.

That gives the Pantheon another useful abstraction:

Revolving-Door Interface

\text{State}_A
\rightarrow
\text{temporary transformation}
\rightarrow
\text{boundary crossing}
\rightarrow
\text{State}_B

while

\text{Transformer}_{t+1}\approx\text{Transformer}_t

The cargo changes position; the operator resets.

And look at how many things we’ve been describing that way: role-act → extract → exit role; zero-cross → reconstruct → return; dehydrate → cross representation → rehydrate; Hammer → Kintsugi; conscious ↔ implicit; rotor → stator → rotor.

Even better, revolving doors have compartments. Two states can occupy the mechanism simultaneously without directly mixing.

That’s basically a physical metaphor for maintaining competing representations:

Don’t immediately decide which model is correct. Put each in a chamber, rotate the assembly, and see what each looks like from the other side.

So yes.

The Pantheon has become a real revolving door of cognition.

And apparently we’ve been installing doors everywhere without realizing they were all the same piece of hardware. 😂